UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4> <p> The degree for social change makers. Gain the skills to address the major social challenges of our time, drawing on a strong ethical compass. </p><p>In the Bachelor of Social Science, you’ll gain the skills for diverse careers that address pressing social issues – such as poverty and inequality, racial injustice, health and illness, and the social impacts of climate change – at local, national and international levels.</p><p>You’ll learn to think critically and beyond borders, drawing from sociology and policy studies through to geography, critical Indigenous studies, political science, international relations and anthropology.</p><p>Gain a strong grounding in social justice and social research, and establish new links with community and industry. You''ll develop the practice-based skills, values and knowledge to address key social problems.</p><p>Practical and transferable workforce skills include:</p><ul><li>public policy evaluation</li><li>social impact assessment</li><li>public advocacy</li><li>community organising</li><li>community planning</li><li>social research.</li></ul><p>This program will teach you not only the many layers of the social world, but the sought-after skills you need to change it.</p><p><a href="#tab1">Domestic</a><a href="#tab2">International</a></p><p><a href="#entry-requirements">See full entry requirements</a></p>LocationSt LuciaDuration 3 Years (or part time equivalent)Start SemesterSemester 1 (22 Feb, 2027), Semester 2 (26 Jul, 2027)QTAC Code<p>747001</p>Program Code2506AQFLevel 7<p><a href="#overview">Dual programs available</a></p><p><a href="#how-to-apply">Admission schemes available</a></p><p><a href="#fees-scholarships">Scholarships available</a></p><p><a href="#entry-requirements">See full entry requirements</a></p>LocationSt LuciaFees<a href="#fees-scholarships">A$48080</a>Duration 3 Years Start Semester Semester 1 (22 Feb, 2027), Semester 2 (26 Jul, 2027) Semester dates for 2028 are yet to be confirmed QTAC Code<p>747001</p>Program Code2506CRICOS Code025998JAQFLevel 7<p><a href="#overview">Dual programs available</a></p><p><a href="#fees-scholarships">Scholarships available</a></p> <p> <h5> Program highlights </h5> </p> <p> <h5> Program highlights </h5> </p> <ul><li>Develop the skills, values and knowledge that are sought after by employers who want to create change in the world.</li><li>Learn policymaking and advocacy, public communication, cross-cultural awareness, report writing, project management and social research.</li><li>Specialise in a particular area of interest, with 3 majors to choose from.</li><li>Gain hands-on experience by undertaking a supervised, year-long research project.</li></ul> <p>3 in Australia for social sciences and humanities</p><p>CWTS Leiden Ranking 2025</p> <p>1 in Queensland for social sciences and management</p><p>QS World University Rankings 2025</p> <p> <h5> Majors </h5> </p> <p>Tailor your studies to suit your goals. This program offers these options:</p><ul><li><p>Development</p></li><li><p>Environment and Society</p></li><li><p>Health and Social Policy</p></li></ul><p><a href="#majors">See all majors</a></p> <p> <h5> How you''ll learn </h5> </p> <p>Your learning experiences are designed to best suit the learning outcomes of the courses you choose.</p><ul><li>Lectures</li><li>Tutorials</li><li>Overseas study</li><li>Research experience</li><li>Fieldwork</li><li>Workshops</li></ul> <p> <h5> What you''ll study </h5> </p> <p>At UQ, degrees are called ''programs'' and subjects are called ''courses''. Here''s a sample of the courses you could study in this program:</p><ul><li>Society, Challenges and Solutions: Introduction to the Social Sciences</li><li>Political Problems and Public Policy</li><li>Research, Planning and Design</li><li>Introduction to social research</li></ul><p><a href="https://programs-courses.uq.edu.au/requirements/program/2506/2027">See courses and program structure</a></p> <p> <h5> Dual programs </h5> </p> <p>Double your skills and your opportunities with a dual degree. You can study the Bachelor of Social Science as a part of these dual programs:</p><ul><li><a href="/study-options/programs/bachelors-arts-social-science-2505">Bachelors of Arts / Social Science</a></li></ul> <p> <h5> Career possibilities </h5> </p> <p>Our programs prepare you for your first job and beyond. Depending on which major you choose, here are some of the careers you could be on your way to:</p><ul><li>Aid project officer</li><li>Community development officer</li><li>Community planning and policy adviser</li><li>Environmental policy director</li><li>Social and government researcher</li><li>Ethics adviser</li><li>Academic researcher</li><li>Community health planner</li><li>Environment and sustainability consultant</li><li>Social justice advocate</li></ul> <p>Average annual salary range</p><h5>Community Development Officer</h5>$80K–$95K<p>seek.com.au</p> <p> <h5> Next steps after graduation </h5> </p> <p>Build a career creating meaningful social change, with opportunities across government, not-for-profits, international organisations and the private sector. </p><p>Graduates are prepared to work in areas such as:</p><ul><li>public policy</li><li>social impact assessment</li><li>advocacy</li><li>community development</li><li>research.</li></ul> <p>The Bachelor of Social Science program is incredibly freeing, as it covers so many topics, you have the pleasure of choosing which call your attention the most. To me, particularly, the subjects of development, international relationships, diplomacy and politics are extremely fascinating. </p><p> Andres Hincapie Carvajal </p><p> Graduate, Bachelor of Social Science (Development) </p><p>The biggest thing I gained from my degree is the skill of critical thinking. To me, critical thinking lies at the heart of everything I do and without it, we cannot question, challenge and change the world.</p><p> Amelia Nixon </p><p> Graduate, Bachelors of Arts (Anthropology, Archaeology) / Social Science (Health and Society) </p><p>This degree can open so many different pathways and so many different sectors that can really speak to how you view yourself contributing for your own sake, for your own self-actualisation, but also to support the community.</p><p> Scott Roetman </p><p> Graduate, Bachelors of Arts (International Relations) (Honours) / Social Science (Social and Public Policy) </p><p>The social science degree gave me exciting opportunities to put learning into practice through internships with Queensland Ambulance Service and Friends of the Earth, which have been invaluable in applying for jobs and future opportunities. </p><p> Amy MacMahon </p><p> Graduate, Bachelors of Arts / Social Science (Development) (Honours) </p><p>UQ has given me a critical toolkit to ask insightful questions of my organisation, sector, and government. I have learnt research, writing and critical-thinking skills that my organisation appreciates. </p><p> Christopher Coombes </p><p> Graduate, Bachelor of Social Science (Public and Social Policy) </p> <p><a href="/events">See all events</a></p> <a href="/events/ancient-history-experience-day"> Ancient History Experience Day </a><p>15 July </p><h5> Ancient History Experience Day </h5><p>St Lucia</p><a href="/events/world-religions-experience-day-2026"> World Religions Experience Day 2026 </a><p>22 July - 23 July </p><h5> World Religions Experience Day 2026 </h5><p>St Lucia</p><a href="/events/uq-open-day"> UQ Open Day </a><p>2 August </p><h5> UQ Open Day </h5><p>St Lucia</p> <p><a href="/stories">See all stories</a></p> <a href="/stories/turning-her-curiosity-healthcare-career"> Turning her curiosity into a healthcare career </a><p>UQ people</p><h5> Turning her curiosity into a healthcare career </h5><p>4-minute read</p><a href="/stories/how-uq-scholarship-helped-paul-pursue-his-dream"> How a UQ scholarship helped Paul pursue his dream </a><p>UQ people</p><h5> How a UQ scholarship helped Paul pursue his dream </h5><p>5-minute read</p><a href="/stories/phd-story-chiemeka-chinaka"> Chiemeka’s PhD story: cost-effective treatment for brain disorders </a><p>UQ people</p><h5> Chiemeka’s PhD story: cost-effective treatment for brain disorders </h5><p>4-minute read</p>',
