UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4> <p> Explore your love of wildlife as you gain expertise in animal biology, conservation and management. </p><p>In this program, you''ll be taught by leading wildlife scientists and biologists, and develop the skills to create and evaluate wildlife management programs for captive and wild animals.</p><p>You''ll study:</p><ul><li>native and exotic amphibians</li><li>reptiles, birds and mammals</li><li>biodiversity</li><li>human-wildlife interactions.</li></ul><p>Gain hands-on wildlife experience in UQ’s wildlife research facilities and have access to some of the best animal and veterinary facilities in the Southern Hemisphere.</p><p>Undertake an industry placement in zoos, sanctuaries, government agencies, welfare organisations or wildlife parks.</p><p>Graduate with the knowledge to explore a wide range of employment opportunities in wildlife management and conservation, or go on to pursue a research career with an <a href="/study-options/programs/bachelor-wildlife-science-honours-2419?year=2027">honours year</a>, which could lead to postgraduate studies.</p><p><a href="#tab1">Domestic</a><a href="#tab2">International</a></p><p><a href="#entry-requirements">See full entry requirements</a></p>LocationGattonDuration 3 Years (or part time equivalent)Start SemesterSemester 1 (22 Feb, 2027)QTAC Code<p>787209</p>Program Code2388AQFLevel 7<p><a href="#how-to-apply">Admission schemes available</a></p><p><a href="#fees-scholarships">Scholarships available</a></p><p><a href="#entry-requirements">See full entry requirements</a></p>LocationGattonFees<a href="#fees-scholarships">A$56800</a>Duration 3 Years Start Semester Semester 1 (22 Feb, 2027) Semester dates for 2028 are yet to be confirmed QTAC Code<p>787209</p>Program Code2388CRICOS Code087888BAQFLevel 7<p><a href="#fees-scholarships">Scholarships available</a></p> <p> <h5> Program highlights </h5> </p> <p> <h5> Program highlights </h5> </p> <ul><li>Gain hands-on experience in some of the best specialist animal research facilities in the Southern Hemisphere.</li><li>Get involved in the conservation and management of wild and captive animals in Australia and overseas.</li><li>Participate in industry placements in zoos, sanctuaries, government agencies, welfare organisations and wildlife parks.</li></ul> <p>1 in Australia for environmental sciences</p><p>QS World University Rankings 2026</p> <p>18 in the world for environmental sciences</p><p>QS World University Rankings 2026</p> <p> <h5> How you''ll learn </h5> </p> <p>Your learning experiences are designed to best suit the learning outcomes of the courses you choose.</p><ul><li>Lectures</li><li>Tutorials</li><li>Work placements</li><li>Research experience</li><li>Laboratory work</li><li>Fieldwork</li><li>Seminars</li><li>Workshops</li></ul> <p> <h5> What you''ll study </h5> </p> <p>At UQ, degrees are called ''programs'' and subjects are called ''courses''. Here''s a sample of the courses you could study in this program:</p><ul><li>Australian Vertebrates [Core course]</li><li>Human Wildlife Interactions [Core course]</li><li>Wildlife Population Management [Core course]</li><li>Animal &amp; Plant Biosecurity [Elective]</li><li>Biodiversity &amp; Systematics [Elective]</li><li>Conservation Planning &amp; Management [Elective]</li></ul><p><a href="https://programs-courses.uq.edu.au/requirements/program/2388/2027">See courses and program structure</a></p> <p> <h5> Career possibilities </h5> </p> <p>Our programs prepare you for your first job and beyond. Here are some of the careers you could be on your way to:</p><ul><li>Biologist</li><li>Wildlife scientist</li><li>Ecotourism guide</li><li>Conservation officer</li><li>Land manager</li><li>Wildlife officer</li><li>Ranger</li><li>Environmental assessment officer</li><li>Aquarist</li><li>Animal research scientist</li></ul> <p>Average annual salary range</p><h5>Ecologist</h5>$90K–$110K<p>seek.com.au</p><p>Average annual salary range</p><h5>Zoologist</h5>$95K–$115K<p>seek.com.au</p> <p> <h5> Next steps after graduation </h5> </p> <p>You''ll find a diverse range of career opportunities in fields or organisations such as:</p><ul><li>ecotourism</li><li>land management</li><li>environmental management</li><li>research</li><li>vertebrae pest and game management</li><li>government departments or agencies</li><li>wildlife sanctuaries and zoos.</li></ul><p><a href="http://career-profiles.science.uq.edu.au/">Find out more about careers that started in science</a></p> <p>I’m passionate about trying to help people coexist with and respect our wildlife. I enjoyed the practical and applied ‘real world’ coursework and projects as well as learning from experienced experts – academics, veterinary and field workers – to prepare for opportunities in a range of wildlife industries.</p><p> Alayn Clark </p><p> Graduate, Bachelor of Wildlife Science </p> <p><a href="/events">See all events</a></p> <a href="/events/uq-open-day"> UQ Open Day </a><p>2 August </p><h5> UQ Open Day </h5><p>St Lucia</p> <p><a href="/stories">See all stories</a></p> <a href="/stories/wildlife-science-careers"> Why study wildlife conservation? </a><p>Careers</p><h5> Why study wildlife conservation? </h5><p>4-minute read</p><a href="/stories/animal-science-careers"> Animal science careers in Australia </a><p>Careers</p><h5> Animal science careers in Australia </h5><p>4-minute read</p><a href="/stories/10-awesome-reasons-study-gatton"> 10 awesome reasons to study at Gatton </a><p>UQ people</p><h5> 10 awesome reasons to study at Gatton </h5><p>6-minute read</p>',
    total_course_duration = '3 Years (or part time equivalent)',
    offshore_tuition_fee = 170400,
    onshore_tuition_fee = 17760,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<h4>Entry Requirements</h4> <p> <h5> Prerequisites </h5> </p> Queensland Year 12 (or equivalent):<ul><li>General English subject (Units 3 &amp; 4, C)</li><li>General Mathematics or Mathematical Methods (Units 3 &amp; 4, C). </li></ul>Biology, Chemistry and/or Physics are recommended subjects. <h5> Minimum entry score </h5> <p>Select where you studied and your qualification to see the minimum entry score you need to be considered for this program.</p><p>Use the minimum entry score as a guide. Your score must be at least equivalent to the required Australian Year 12 ATAR score. Entry requirements are subject to change.</p> <h5>Equivalent subjects</h5> <table><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Qualification equivalent</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table><p> <h5> English language requirements </h5> </p> <p>IELTS overall 6.5; reading 6; writing 6; speaking 6; listening 6. For other English Language Proficiency Tests and Scores approved for UQ</p><p>TOEFL iBT (including Paper Edition) - Overall 87, listening 19, reading 19, writing 21 and speaking 19.</p><p>PTE Academic - Overall Score of 64 and 60 in all sub bands.</p><p>BE - A minimum overall grade of 4 plus a minimum grade of C in all macro skills.</p><p>CES - Overall 176 and 169 in all sub bands.</p><p>OET is not accepted.</p><p>There are other ways to meet the English language requirements. For some programs, additional conditions apply.</p><p><a href="/admissions/english-language-requirements">Learn how to meet the English language requirements</a></p> <p> <h5> Student visas </h5> </p> <p>International students who are accepted into full-time study in the Bachelor of Wildlife Science are eligible to apply for an Australian student visa (subclass 500).</p><p>There are a number of requirements you must satisfy before a visa is granted, including the Genuine Student (GS) requirement.</p><p><a href="/university-life/getting-prepared-to-come-to-australia/student-visas">Learn more about student visas</a></p> <p> <h5> Need help meeting the entry requirements? </h5> </p> <p>We can help you meet the minimum entry score, subject prerequisites or English language requirements for your preferred program.</p><p><a href="/admissions/undergraduate/consider-your-pathway-options">Learn about pathway options</a></p> <p> <h5> Additional application information </h5> </p> <p> You''ll be exposed to animals and agricultural environments as part of your learning. Immunisation against Q fever is mandatory and you''ll be required to provide evidence of immunisation upon commencement of the program. Q fever screening and vaccination is currently only available in Australia. Refer to the <a href="https://my.uq.edu.au/information-and-services/manage-my-program/immunisation-and-disease-screening/student-immunisation-requirements/animal-agricultural">UQ Student Immunisation Requirements</a> for more information. </p>',
    apply_form = 'https://study.uq.edu.au/study-options/programs/bachelor-wildlife-science-2388',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '087888B';

UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4> <p> Further your passion for native fauna by extending your wildlife science expertise with an honours year. </p><p>The Bachelor of Wildlife Science (Honours) is an additional year of study following on from the <a href="/study-options/programs/bachelor-wildlife-science-2388?year=2027">Bachelor of Wildlife Science</a>.</p><p>Undertake an independent research project within the wildlife science field under the supervision of a wildlife-focused academic staff member.</p><p>You''ll gain skills in research methodologies, research communication and entrepreneurship, which will enable you to continue into a research higher degree or move into a diverse range of employment.</p><p>The honours year builds on the knowledge gained through the wildlife science undergraduate program, including study of:</p><ul><li>native and exotic amphibians</li><li>reptiles, birds and mammals</li><li>biodiversity</li><li>human-wildlife interactions.</li></ul><p>With this extended program, you''ll develop further expertise in wildlife management programs for captive and free-ranging wildlife. You''ll be able to make a major contribution to the wildlife, game and vertebrate pest management industries in Australia and overseas.</p><p><a href="#tab1">Domestic</a><a href="#tab2">International</a></p><p><a href="#entry-requirements">See full entry requirements</a></p><p>LocationGattonDuration 1 Year full-time (or part-time equivalent) Start SemesterSemester 1 (22 Feb, 2027), Semester 2 (26 Jul, 2027)Program Code2419AQFLevel 8</p><p><a href="#fees-scholarships">Scholarships available</a></p><p><a href="#entry-requirements">See full entry requirements</a></p><p>LocationGattonFees<a href="#fees-scholarships">A$56800</a>Duration 1 Year full-time Start Semester Semester 1 (22 Feb, 2027), Semester 2 (26 Jul, 2027) Semester dates for 2028 are yet to be confirmed Program Code2419CRICOS Code092447GAQFLevel 8</p><p><a href="#fees-scholarships">Scholarships available</a></p> <p> <h5> Program highlights </h5> </p> <p> <h5> Program highlights </h5> </p> <ul><li>Build on your Bachelor of Wildlife Science with an independent research project within the wildlife science field.</li><li>Be supported by a wildlife-focused academic staff member at some of the best specialist animal research facilities in the Southern Hemisphere.</li><li>Gain skills in research methodologies, research communication and entrepreneurship.</li></ul> <p>1 in Australia for environmental sciences</p><p>QS World University Rankings 2026</p> <p>18 in the world for environmental sciences</p><p>QS World University Rankings 2026</p> <p> <h5> How you''ll learn </h5> </p> <p>Your learning experiences are designed to best suit the learning outcomes of the courses you choose.</p><ul><li>Research experience</li><li>Laboratory work</li><li>Fieldwork</li></ul> <p> <h5> What you''ll study </h5> </p> <p>At UQ, degrees are called ''programs'' and subjects are called ''courses''. Here''s a sample of the courses you could study in this program:</p><ul><li>Research Methods [Core course] </li><li>Honours Research Project [Core course]</li><li>Biodiversity &amp; Systematics [Elective]</li><li>Vertebrate Diversity &amp; Evolution [Elective]</li><li>Risk Management [Elective]</li><li>Science Engagement in the Community [Elective]</li><li>Data Science for Life and Environmental Scientists [Elective]</li></ul><p><a href="https://programs-courses.uq.edu.au/requirements/program/2419/2027">See courses and program structure</a></p> <p> <h5> Career possibilities </h5> </p> <p>Our programs prepare you for your first job and beyond. Here are some of the careers you could be on your way to:</p><ul><li>Biologist</li><li>Wildlife scientist</li><li>Ecotourism guide</li><li>Conservation officer</li><li>Land manager</li><li>Wildlife officer</li><li>Ranger</li><li>Environmental assessment officer</li><li>Aquarist</li></ul> <p>Average annual salary range</p><h5>Ecologist</h5>$90K–$110K<p>seek.com.au</p><p>Average annual salary range</p><h5>Zoologist</h5>$95K–$115K<p>seek.com.au</p> <p> <h5> Next steps after graduation </h5> </p> <p>You''ll find a diverse range of career opportunities in fields or organisations such as:</p><ul><li>ecotourism</li><li>land management</li><li>environmental management</li><li>research</li><li>vertebrate pest and game management</li><li>government departments or agencies</li><li>wildlife sanctuaries and zoos.</li></ul><p><a href="http://career-profiles.science.uq.edu.au/">Find out more about careers that started in science</a></p> <p>I’m passionate about trying to help people coexist with and respect our wildlife. I enjoyed the practical and applied ‘real world’ coursework and projects as well as learning from experienced experts – academics, veterinary and field workers – to prepare for opportunities in a range of wildlife industries.</p><p> Alayn Clark </p><p> Graduate, Bachelor of Wildlife Science </p> <p><a href="/events">See all events</a></p> <a href="/events/uq-open-day"> UQ Open Day </a><p>2 August </p><h5> UQ Open Day </h5><p>St Lucia</p> <p><a href="/stories">See all stories</a></p> <a href="/stories/animal-science-careers"> Animal science careers in Australia </a><p>Careers</p><h5> Animal science careers in Australia </h5><p>4-minute read</p><a href="/stories/wildlife-science-careers"> Why study wildlife conservation? </a><p>Careers</p><h5> Why study wildlife conservation? </h5><p>4-minute read</p><a href="/stories/10-awesome-reasons-study-gatton"> 10 awesome reasons to study at Gatton </a><p>UQ people</p><h5> 10 awesome reasons to study at Gatton </h5><p>6-minute read</p>',
    total_course_duration = '1 Year full-time (or part-time equivalent)',
    offshore_tuition_fee = 56800,
    onshore_tuition_fee = 9540,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<h4>Entry Requirements</h4> <h5>Prerequisites</h5> Bachelor of Wildlife Science (or equivalent), with:<ul><li>a grade point average (GPA) of 5.0 on a 7-point scale, and</li><li>have secured an honours project and supervisor, and</li><li>satisfied any additional requirements set by the head of school.</li></ul> <p> <h5> Minimum entry score </h5> </p> <p>Select where you studied and your qualification to see the minimum entry score you need to be considered for this program.</p><p>Use the minimum entry score as a guide. Your score must be at least equivalent to the required Australian Year 12 ATAR score. Entry requirements are subject to change.</p> <h5>Equivalent subjects</h5> <table><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Qualification equivalent</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table><p> <h5> English language requirements </h5> </p> <p>IELTS overall 6.5; reading 6; writing 6; speaking 6; listening 6. For other English Language Proficiency Tests and Scores approved for UQ</p><p>TOEFL iBT (including Paper Edition) - Overall 87, listening 19, reading 19, writing 21 and speaking 19.</p><p>PTE Academic - Overall Score of 64 and 60 in all sub bands.</p><p>BE - A minimum overall grade of 4 plus a minimum grade of C in all macro skills.</p><p>CES - Overall 176 and 169 in all sub bands.</p><p>OET is not accepted.</p><p>There are other ways to meet the English language requirements. For some programs, additional conditions apply.</p><p><a href="/admissions/english-language-requirements">Learn how to meet the English language requirements</a></p> <p> <h5> Student visas </h5> </p> <p>International students who are accepted into full-time study in the Bachelor of Wildlife Science (Honours) are eligible to apply for an Australian student visa (subclass 500).</p><p>There are a number of requirements you must satisfy before a visa is granted, including the Genuine Student (GS) requirement.</p><p><a href="/university-life/getting-prepared-to-come-to-australia/student-visas">Learn more about student visas</a></p>',
    apply_form = 'https://study.uq.edu.au/study-options/programs/bachelor-wildlife-science-honours-2419',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '092447G';

UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4> <p> Develop professional writing and editing skills across print and digital formats. </p><p>The digitisation of the modern economy has created a raft of new job titles, all of which require expertise in writing and editing – in addition to the jobs that already drive Australia’s dynamic creative industries. Gain the writing, editing, publishing, design and research skills to build a rewarding career in print or online.</p><p>The Graduate Certificate in Writing, Editing and Publishing covers all stages of the writing and publishing process, and the skills needed to meet growing demands. During this half-year full-time (or part-time equivalent) program, you''ll develop practical knowledge and experience in writing and editing across genres, including:</p><ul><li>creative writing</li><li>corporate and technical documents</li><li>academic research in the creative arts</li><li>advertising and marketing.</li></ul><p>Your lecturers will include award-winning authors and arts industry professionals, as well as an array of guest lecturers with expertise in all aspects of professional writing and publishing. Together, they''ll provide you with up-to-date insights and industry knowledge across traditional and new media.</p><p>You’ll learn about the continually evolving landscapes of the Australian and international publishing industries, and have the opportunity to intern at University of Queensland Press, literary journals, educational publishers and trade publishers, including UQ’s own Corella Press, which specialises in rediscovering 19th-century crime and mystery writers.</p><p><a href="#tab1">Domestic</a><a href="#tab2">International</a></p><p><a href="#entry-requirements">See full entry requirements</a></p><p>LocationSt LuciaDuration Half Year (or part time equivalent)Start SemesterSemester 1 (22 Feb, 2027), Semester 2 (26 Jul, 2027)Program Code5678AQFLevel 8</p><p><a href="#fees-scholarships">Scholarships available</a></p><p><a href="#entry-requirements">See full entry requirements</a></p><p>LocationSt LuciaFees<a href="#fees-scholarships">A$24040</a>Duration Half Year Start Semester Semester 1 (22 Feb, 2027), Semester 2 (26 Jul, 2027) Semester dates for 2028 are yet to be confirmed Program Code5678CRICOS Code093730GAQFLevel 8</p><p><a href="#fees-scholarships">Scholarships available</a></p> <p> <h5> Program highlights </h5> </p> <p> <h5> Program highlights </h5> </p> <ul><li>Learn from award-winning authors and arts industry professionals.</li><li>Develop practical knowledge and experience in writing and editing across a range of genres.</li><li>Intern at highly respected journals and publishing houses.</li><li>Gain the qualifications needed to meet the growing demand for mastery of the written word.</li></ul> <p>1 in Queensland for arts and humanities</p><p>QS World University Rankings 2026</p> <p>1 in Queensland for arts and humanities</p><p>Times Higher Education World University Rankings 2026</p> <p> <h5> How you''ll learn </h5> </p> <p>Your learning experiences are designed to best suit the learning outcomes of the courses you choose.</p><ul><li>Lectures</li><li>Tutorials</li><li>Work placements</li><li>Research experience</li><li>Seminars</li></ul> <p> <h5> What you''ll study </h5> </p> <p>At UQ, degrees are called ''programs'' and subjects are called ''courses''. Here''s a sample of the courses you could study in this program:</p><ul><li>Publishing and Professional Practice</li><li>Writing Creative Non-Fiction [Elective]</li><li>Fundamentals of Advertising [Elective]</li><li>Industry Internship [Elective]</li><li>Fiction, Ideas, Creativity [Elective]</li><li>Editing the Manuscript [Elective]</li></ul><p><a href="https://programs-courses.uq.edu.au/requirements/program/5678/2027">See courses and program structure</a></p> <p> <h5> Career possibilities </h5> </p> <p>Postgraduate study can take you anywhere. Here are some of the careers you could be on your way to:</p><ul><li>Author</li><li>Editorial writer</li><li>Editor</li><li>Book editor</li><li>Content creator</li><li>Content producer</li><li>Copywriter</li><li>Publishing coordinator</li><li>Sub-editor</li><li>Online publisher</li></ul> <p>Average annual salary range</p><h5>Editor</h5>$115–$135K<p>seek.com.au</p> <p> <h5> Next steps after graduation </h5> </p> <p>Our graduates have built rewarding careers in publishing, editing and professional writing, with some going on to become best-selling authors and creatives.</p> <p><a href="/events">See all events</a></p> <a href="/events/ancient-history-experience-day"> Ancient History Experience Day </a><p>15 July </p><h5> Ancient History Experience Day </h5><p>St Lucia</p><a href="/events/world-religions-experience-day-2026"> World Religions Experience Day 2026 </a><p>22 July - 23 July </p><h5> World Religions Experience Day 2026 </h5><p>St Lucia</p><a href="/events/uq-open-day"> UQ Open Day </a><p>2 August </p><h5> UQ Open Day </h5><p>St Lucia</p> <p><a href="/stories">See all stories</a></p> <a href="/stories/enduring-value-commerce-degree"> The enduring value of a commerce degree </a><p>Careers</p><h5> The enduring value of a commerce degree </h5><p>5-minute read</p><a href="/stories/which-advanced-business-program-right-me"> Which advanced business program is right for me? </a><p>Study tips</p><h5> Which advanced business program is right for me? </h5><p>8-minute read</p><a href="/stories/phd-story-pierce-wilcox"> Pierce’s PhD story: storytelling for a better world </a><p>UQ people</p><h5> Pierce’s PhD story: storytelling for a better world </h5><p>6-minute read</p>',
    total_course_duration = '',
    offshore_tuition_fee = 24040,
    onshore_tuition_fee = 15600,
    enrolment_fee = NULL,
    materials_fee = NULL,
    entry_requirements = '<h4>Entry Requirements</h4> <h5>Entry requirements</h5> <p>To be eligible for entry, you''ll need:</p> <ul><li>an applicant must submit a ten page portfolio for the review of the Program Convenor (see below), and have either</li><li>a bachelor''s degree (or equivalent) in any discipline, or</li><li>have completed post-secondary study, or</li><li>2 years full-time equivalent relevant work experience (see below).</li></ul><br/>You must have a grade point average (GPA) of 4.0 on a 7-point scale in your previous qualification. <p> <h5> Relevant disciplines for previous qualifications </h5> </p> <p>Relevant disciplines include English, English literature, writing, journalism, communication, art history, history, law as well as other relevant fields from the humanities and social sciences.</p><p>You must have completed a minimum amount of content in a relevant discipline, this would be at least a major, field of study, or approximately 30% of program content in the discipline, including a mix of introductory and advanced courses.</p> <p> <h5> Relevant work experience </h5> </p> <p>Relevant work experience includes work using writing and editing in a professional or volunteer context. Relevant work experience includes work in: government departments, office management, corporate communications, administration, journalism, and public relations, among others, which should be supported with evidence outlined below. </p><p>Evidence of relevant work experience should include a letter from your employer (and/or previous employers) stating the following: </p><ul><li>that you work (or worked) within the specified organisation </li><li>the nature of your work, including any relevant duties and responsibilities</li><li>the length that you were there (i.e. 2 years) </li><li>the level at which you worked (full-time, part-time or casual). If part-time or casual, please list the average amount of hours worked per week. </li></ul> <p> <h5> GPA equivalent </h5> </p> <p>Select where you studied and your qualification to see the GPA equivalent you need to be considered for this program.</p><p>Use the GPA equivalent as a guide. When you apply, we’ll calculate your GPA using the UQ grading scale. Any failing grades will be included. Entry requirements are subject to change.</p> <h5>Equivalent subjects</h5> <table><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Qualification equivalent</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table><p> <h5> Related programs </h5> </p> <p>Depending on your previous qualifications and current goals, you might want to consider<br/> one of these related programs:</p><ul><li><a href="/study-options/programs/master-writing-editing-and-publishing-5681">Master of Writing, Editing and Publishing</a></li></ul> <p> <h5> English language requirements </h5> </p> <p>IELTS overall 7; reading 7; writing 7; speaking 7; listening 7. For other English Language Proficiency Tests and Scores approved for UQ</p><p>TOEFL iBT (including Paper Edition) - Overall 100, listening 25, reading 25, writing 27, speaking 23.</p><p>PTE Academic - Overall 72, sub bands minimum 72.</p><p>CES - Overall 185, All sub bands minimum 185.</p><p>BE and OET are not accepted.</p><p>There are other ways to meet the English language requirements. For some programs, additional conditions apply.</p><p><a href="/admissions/english-language-requirements">Learn how to meet the English language requirements</a></p> <p> <h5> Student visas </h5> </p> <p>International students who are accepted into full-time study in the Graduate Certificate in Writing, Editing and Publishing are eligible to apply for an Australian student visa (subclass 500).</p><p>There are a number of requirements you must satisfy before a visa is granted, including the Genuine Student (GS) requirement.</p><p><a href="/university-life/getting-prepared-to-come-to-australia/student-visas">Learn more about student visas</a></p> <p> <h5> Additional application information </h5> </p> Applicants are required to submit a portfolio with their application for enrolment and demonstrate an appropriate standard to the satisfaction of the program director. &lt;10&gt;<p>&lt;10&gt;</p><li>The portfolio should include approximately 10 word processed pages of writing (e.g. assignments submitted in previous courses, workplace documents, creative writing manuscripts). </li>&lt;10&gt;<li>This portfolio must be original work of the applicant and in English and completed without the use of AI or Machine Translation (MT) technology. </li>&lt;10&gt;<br/>&lt;10&gt;<p>Portfolio Criteria: </p>&lt;10&gt;<p> The portfolio allows applicants to showcase their writing skills while also demonstrating their readiness for graduate-level study. These criteria are designed to assess current abilities and future potential.</p>&lt;10&gt;<p>1. Quality of Writing</p>&lt;10&gt;<li>Demonstrates strong command of language</li>&lt;10&gt;<li>Competent expression appropriate to the purpose and audience</li>&lt;10&gt;<li>Shows creativity, originality and/or effectiveness in ideas and expression</li>&lt;10&gt;<br/><p>2. Variety of writing samples</p>&lt;10&gt;<li>Demonstrates versatility and range as a writer</li>&lt;10&gt;<li>May include different styles and/or genres of fiction, non-fiction, poetry, academic writing, workplace writing, journalistic pieces, etc. </li><br/>&lt;10&gt;<br/><p>3. Editing skills</p>&lt;10&gt;<li>Demonstrates attention to detail and proofreading abilities</li>&lt;10&gt;<li>Shows familiarity with the most common grammar and punctuation conventions</li><br/>&lt;10&gt;<br/><p>4. Professional presentation</p>&lt;10&gt;<li>Portfolio is well-organised and easy to navigate</li>&lt;10&gt;<li>Writing samples are polished and formatted appropriately</li><br/>&lt;10&gt;<br/><p>5. Potential for growth</p>&lt;10&gt;<li>Shows promise and room for development at the postgraduate level</li><br/>',
    apply_form = 'https://study.uq.edu.au/study-options/programs/graduate-certificate-writing-editing-and-publishing-5678',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE cricos_course_code = '093730G';

UPDATE courses SET
    course_description = '<h4>Overview</h4> <p> Gain the writing, editing, publishing, design and research skills to build a rewarding career in print or online. </p><p>This program covers all stages of the writing and publishing process, and the skills needed to meet growing demands.</p><p>You''ll develop practical knowledge and experience in writing and editing across genres, including:</p><ul><li>creative writing</li><li>corporate and technical documents</li><li>academic research in the creative arts</li><li>advertising, marketing and more.</li></ul><p>You''ll be taught by award-winning authors and arts industry professionals, as well as guest lecturers with expertise in all aspects of professional writing and publishing. Together, they''ll provide you with up-to-date insights and industry knowledge across traditional and new media.</p><p>You’ll learn about the evolving landscapes of the Australian and international publishing industries, and have the opportunity to intern at a variety of arts organisations. Hone your skills at University of Queensland Press, literary journals, educational and trade publishers, including UQ’s own <a href="https://www.austlit.edu.au/corellapress">Corella Press</a>, which specialises in rediscovering 19th-century crime and mystery writers.</p><p>The program provides a strong foundation for further research or creative practice. Refine your voice and contribute to Australia’s and the global publishing landscape.</p><p><a href="#tab1">Domestic</a><a href="#tab2">International</a></p><p><a href="#entry-requirements">See full entry requirements</a></p><p>LocationSt LuciaDuration 1 Year full-time (or part-time equivalent) 1.5 Years full-time (or part-time equivalent) Start SemesterSemester 1 (22 Feb, 2027), Semester 2 (26 Jul, 2027)Program Code5681AQFLevel 9</p><p><a href="#fees-scholarships">Scholarships available</a></p><p><a href="#entry-requirements">See full entry requirements</a></p><p>LocationSt LuciaFees<a href="#fees-scholarships">A$48080</a>Duration 1 Year full-time (only available as full-time study) 1.5 Years full-time (only available as full-time study) Start Semester Semester 1 (22 Feb, 2027), Semester 2 (26 Jul, 2027) Semester dates for 2028 are yet to be confirmed Program Code5681CRICOS Code093732EAQFLevel 9</p><p><a href="#fees-scholarships">Scholarships available</a></p> <p> <h5> Program highlights </h5> </p> <p> <h5> Program highlights </h5> </p> <ul><li>Learn from award-winning authors and arts industry professionals.</li><li>Develop practical knowledge and experience in writing and editing across a range of genres.</li><li>Intern at highly respected journals and publishing houses.</li><li>Graduate with the qualifications needed to meet the growing demand for mastery of the written word.</li></ul> <p>1 in Queensland for arts and humanities</p><p>QS World University Rankings 2026</p> <p>1 in Queensland for arts and humanities</p><p>Times Higher Education World University Rankings 2026</p> <p> <h5> How you''ll learn </h5> </p> <p>Your learning experiences are designed to best suit the learning outcomes of the courses you choose.</p><ul><li>Lectures</li><li>Tutorials</li><li>Work placements</li><li>Research experience</li><li>Seminars</li></ul> <p> <h5> What you''ll study </h5> </p> <p>At UQ, degrees are called ''programs'' and subjects are called ''courses''. Here''s a sample of the courses you could study in this program:</p><ul><li>Publishing and Professional Practice [Core course]</li><li>Digital Storytelling [Elective]</li><li>Workplace Writing and Editing [Elective]</li><li>Writing and Publishing for Children and Young Adults [Elective]</li><li>Fundamentals of Advertising [Elective]</li></ul><p><a href="https://programs-courses.uq.edu.au/requirements/program/5681/2027">See courses and program structure</a></p> <p> <h5> Career possibilities </h5> </p> <p>Postgraduate study can take you anywhere. Here are some of the careers you could be on your way to:</p><ul><li>Author</li><li>Editor</li><li>Editorial writer</li><li>Content writer</li><li>Book editor</li><li>Copywriter</li><li>Digital editor</li><li>Digital content writer</li><li>Publishing executive</li><li>Online publisher</li></ul> <p>Average annual salary range</p><h5>Editor</h5>$115–$135K<p>seek.com.au</p> <p> <h5> Next steps after graduation </h5> </p> <p>Graduates can pursue roles across the creative industries, media, government and corporate sectors. Apply your expertise in content creation, editorial practice and publishing to engage audiences in meaningful ways across print and digital platforms.</p><p>As your career develops, you may choose to specialise in areas such as literary publishing, professional writing, content strategy or communications, or build a portfolio career that spans multiple industries.</p><p>Graduates have built rewarding careers in publishing, editing and professional writing, with some going on to become best-selling authors and creatives.</p> <p>Writing has been a gateway for me to represent myself (and my people) in literature, to hopefully show other First Nations people they belong in books and adventures, too. My biggest achievement has been writing children''s books for Ash Barty.</p><p> Jasmin McGaughey </p><p> Graduate, Master of Writing, Editing and Publishing </p><p>This program offers graduates a wide range of opportunities to pursue freelance or full-time careers in writing, editing and publishing. I learned so much during my master''s, both from the lecturers and industry experts, but also from the amazing students I got to meet.</p><p> Shastra Deo </p><p> Graduate, Master of Writing, Editing and Publishing </p><p>The wide-reaching scope of the courses means I''ve been able to work not only in the arts, but in the corporate world globally. </p><p> Beth Barber </p><p> Graduate, Master of Writing, Editing and Publishing </p><p>My degree allowed me to follow my passions whilst earning a set of skills that translate to many industries.</p><p> Camille Layt </p><p> Graduate, Master of Writing, Editing and Publishing </p> <p><a href="/events">See all events</a></p> <a href="/events/ancient-history-experience-day"> Ancient History Experience Day </a><p>15 July </p><h5> Ancient History Experience Day </h5><p>St Lucia</p><a href="/events/world-religions-experience-day-2026"> World Religions Experience Day 2026 </a><p>22 July - 23 July </p><h5> World Religions Experience Day 2026 </h5><p>St Lucia</p><a href="/events/uq-open-day"> UQ Open Day </a><p>2 August </p><h5> UQ Open Day </h5><p>St Lucia</p> <p><a href="/stories">See all stories</a></p> <a href="/stories/enduring-value-commerce-degree"> The enduring value of a commerce degree </a><p>Careers</p><h5> The enduring value of a commerce degree </h5><p>5-minute read</p><a href="/stories/which-advanced-business-program-right-me"> Which advanced business program is right for me? </a><p>Study tips</p><h5> Which advanced business program is right for me? </h5><p>8-minute read</p><a href="/stories/phd-story-pierce-wilcox"> Pierce’s PhD story: storytelling for a better world </a><p>UQ people</p><h5> Pierce’s PhD story: storytelling for a better world </h5><p>6-minute read</p>',
    total_course_duration = '1 Year full-time (or part-time equivalent) 1.5 Years full-time (or part-time equivalent)',
    offshore_tuition_fee = 48080,
